  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA weak upper low continues spinning over the northern Ohio\nValley this afternoon as an inverted surface trough gradually\nshifts west across northern Ohio. These features continue to\nproduce scattered showers this afternoon, especially across\nNorth Central OH. The upper low will fill and shift to the\nsoutheast tonight into Thursday as easterly low-level flow\nadvects in drier air. This will cause showers to continue \ngradually shifting west while diminishing and eventually ending \nthrough this evening. Skies are expected to gradually become \nmore clear from east to west through the night, with fog not \nexpected due to a bit of an easterly breeze. Lows tonight will \nrange from the 40s across parts of interior Northeast \nOH/Northwest PA to the low to mid 50s across Northwest OH and \nnear the Lake Erie shoreline. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nHigh pressure will build into the Great Lakes Thursday and\nFriday, persisting into early next week bringing a prolonged\nperiod of tranquil/dry weather. The next chance for rain appears\nto be Wednesday of next week. Below normal temperatures through\nMonday will begin rebounding on Tuesday as we get into return\nflow behind the departing high. Locations away from Lake Erie\nwill see several nights with lows likely dipping into the 40s\nthrough early next week. The coolest nights will be Thursday and\nFriday nights. It is possible that a few more sheltered valleys\neast of I-79 in Northwest PA dip into the upper 30s Thursday\nnight, along with perhaps in some isolated spots across \ninterior Northeast Ohio and around the Central Highlands. Dew \npoints look a few degrees too warm to really be concerned about \na widespread or impactful frost Thursday night/early Friday so\ndo not have any included in the forecast at the moment.",
